  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/4d4353286b5d1668d2a67539db8b1fd27227872b1f5a2eaa5fd6015716f255b0.png)\n\nDetermine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)$ (shown above) is invertible.",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-3,4).$\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in [1,4).$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=-1$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/a709558673202ef4a7835dcea6e23b7ec5b13684272c56023058223c01fb8061.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in [-3,1].$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/5a65e71f571e9510580ba5fe105a6eac48acfe4564c5a6bf76371c2bf09ff651.png)\n\nTherefore, the largest interval on which the function is invertible is:\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1]\n$$",
